REASON: I cannot determine whether every URL in the repository is accessible (non-404) or archived because confirming that requires making network requests (HTTP GET/HEAD to each URL and/or queries to the Wayback Machine API). Per the operating rules I may not execute network requests, so I cannot obtain the runtime evidence needed to PASS or FAIL the test.

EVIDENCE:
- Repo search shows only one explicit HTTP URL in the text source: [repo/arxiv.tex:8-8] contains the comment "http://www.ctan.org/tex-archive/fonts/mathdesign/". (Search for http(s):// in the repo produced no other textual URLs; PDFs in repo/gfx were binary and were checked via pdftotext with no URLs found.)
  - Citation: [/repo/arxiv.tex:8-8]
- Files present that were inspected but contain no textual URLs: 00README.json (source listing), arxiv.bbl (bibliography), and three PDFs under repo/gfx (performance_boxplots_*.pdf). These PDFs were converted to text locally during analysis and contained no http(s) strings.

WHAT IS NEEDED TO RESOLVE (exact steps you can run locally):
1) Enumerate all URLs found in the repository (the current inventory is the single URL above).
2) For each URL, run an HTTP request using Python requests with an explicit User-Agent header and record the status code. Prefer a HEAD request first and fall back to GET if HEAD is not allowed.
   Example Python snippet to run locally (must be executed with network access):

import requests
headers = {"User-Agent": "repo-url-checker/1.0 (+https://your-org.example)"}
urls = ["http://www.ctan.org/tex-archive/fonts/mathdesign/"]
results = {}
for u in urls:
    try:
        r = requests.head(u, allow_redirects=True, timeout=10, headers=headers)
        status = r.status_code
        if status == 405:  # method not allowed, try GET
            r = requests.get(u, allow_redirects=True, timeout=10, headers=headers)
            status = r.status_code
        results[u] = {"status_code": status, "url_after_redirects": r.url}
    except Exception as e:
        results[u] = {"error": str(e)}

# For any URL returning 404 (or other client error), query Wayback:
for u, info in list(results.items()):
    if info.get("status_code") == 404 or "error" in info:
        api = "http://archive.org/wayback/available?url={}".format(u)
        try:
            ra = requests.get(api, timeout=10, headers=headers).json()
            results[u]["wayback"] = ra
        except Exception as e:
            results[u]["wayback_error"] = str(e)

print(results)

3) Provide the recorded results (status_code and Wayback availability JSON) for each URL. If every URL either returns a non-404 HTTP status or the Wayback API indicates an archived snapshot is available, then the test can be marked PASS. If any URL returns 404 and has no Wayback snapshot, the test is FAIL.

If you run the above checks and paste the results here, I will produce a final PASS/FAIL with concrete citations to the HTTP/Wayback responses.

